rs in government  if you have not achieved your  initial objectives it's extremely  unlikely that you are going to  um and all governments uh run out of  steam  of ideas and the ability the capacity to  reinvent themselves and i think  we're seeing a lot of that with nicola  sturgeon's government at the moment as  well  which accounts for some of the sort of  frustration and  this sort of curious sense again as we  got was as we were saying at the  beginning  where on the one hand scottish politics  is extremely stable  you know the snp first everyone else  nowhere and yet within that  there is an enormous amount of strife  and instability  uh and inside the snp and more generally  in scottish politics it is if you like a  sort of  caledonian anticipatory of our times  yes indeed stephen you your doc  crucial documents that should be in the  public domain being  massively redacted crucial submissions  to a  public inquiry not being published  the crown office seemingly using  jigsaw identification with in the  broadest possible interpretation  in ways that sometimes defy logic  to close down information becoming  public that they don't want  to become public if that was happening  in london i couldn't see the the the  london press  london media putting up with this is the  scottish media just too supine  well i i think that there is um first of  all  alex identified some of the important  structural issues you know scotland is  obviously it's a smaller country  we have um if us even proportionally we  probably have um  uh less in the way of a media landscape  um  we you know you could argue the the  media is  less adversarial i'm talking about  broadcast media here  um there is less of a there's there's no  sort of a robert peston or  uh laura kunzberg um sort of figure  who's  um uh you know it is seen as the thorn  in the side of the government of the day  whoever that is  um and also you know there is the the  point as i say as alex makes that the  um the scottis