y  to to press for a second direct second  independence referendum  that brexit is the kind of change in  circumstance that justifies  revisiting the national question six  seven eight years after  it was notionally decided in 2014. and  even if you're opposed to the smp's  plans i think one has to accept that  there is a certain moral force to that  argument  against that however it is only six  seven eight years since  uh the there was a referendum and it is  also i think morally plausible um and  morally just to argue that it is too  soon to reopen that question because  referendums have to have meaning  and that means the results have to be  given meaning otherwise you're into the  never-ending world  which is of little use to most people  and so you have a collision  between plausible arguments it seems to  me  and uh in the absence of consensus that  there should be  a referendum on independence and that  consensus remember  existed in 2012 which allowed the 2014  referendum to take place  in the in the absence of that consensus  it seems to me that it is very difficult  to proceed with one  even if you have the legal right to do  so um  even if the courts confirmed that you  could um because it has to be  a if uh a gold standard referendum to  use the smp's own phrase  and that means replicating most of the  conditions that applied in 2014  and as i say i think that one of the  chief ones of those is the consent of  those  who do not want independence you know  the unions  you could if unionists agreed that there  should be a referendum there would be no  problem  but you know we all have to live in the  scotland that happens  after it and so therefore the referendum  itself i think  has to take place at a time when there  is widespread acceptance and agreement  that it is a suitable moment for it  and we are still some way off that you  know the just say no  approach which the prime minister is  duty-bound to follow  is clearly subject to the laws of  dimini